WEBVTT

1
00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.634
- Hey, lad.
- Hey.

2
00:01:59.635 --> 00:02:00.899
- Is that me?
- Yeah.

3
00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.869
You'd turn up late
for your own funeral, wouldn't ya?

4
00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:05.539
Couldn't find my passport.
Better late tonight than tomorrow.

5
00:02:05.541 --> 00:02:07.040
- Good to see you, man.
- Where's the girls?

6
00:02:07.042 --> 00:02:09.278
In the toilet.
You packed? You all set?

7
00:02:09.279 --> 00:02:10.730
Yeah. You nervous?

8
00:02:10.881 --> 00:02:12.412
- No.
- Good.

9
00:02:12.413 --> 00:02:15.974
- To Kurdistan.
- To Kurdistan. Slainte.

10
00:02:17.518 --> 00:02:19.679
Hey, love.
How are you?

11
00:02:19.855 --> 00:02:22.322
- You packed? Yeah?
- Ready to go.

12
00:02:22.323 --> 00:02:23.916
How's it going?

13
00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:26.393
- Any action?
- God, I hope not.

14
00:02:26.395 --> 00:02:27.861
Running out of time.

15
00:02:27.862 --> 00:02:29.729
- Seat's in the corner. Let's get it.
- Yeah.

16
00:02:29.731 --> 00:02:31.790
Yeah, yeah.

17
00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:36.370
How are you?

18
00:02:36.372 --> 00:02:37.838
It's not my fault.

19
00:02:37.839 --> 00:02:40.499
I'm not pointing a finger.

20
00:02:43.711 --> 00:02:47.080
So, quiet tonight?

21
00:02:47.082 --> 00:02:48.783
Yeah. Think so.

22
00:02:48.785 --> 00:02:50.416
- Think it's sad.
- Sad?

23
00:02:50.417 --> 00:02:53.622
- Yeah, you're going.
- Geez, the guilt.

24
00:02:53.624 --> 00:02:55.823
Ladle it on.

25
00:02:55.824 --> 00:02:58.194
Anyway,
we've decided some names.

26
00:02:58.195 --> 00:02:59.860
- Yeah?
- Mm-hmm.

27
00:02:59.862 --> 00:03:02.718
If it's a girl, she'll be Morna.

28
00:03:03.266 --> 00:03:05.099
- Morna.
- It means "beloved".

29
00:03:05.101 --> 00:03:06.768
- It's nice.
- Yeah.

30
00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:08.055
And if it's a boy?

31
00:03:12.341 --> 00:03:16.643
Well, it was a tough one,
but we finally agreed on Mark.

32
00:03:16.644 --> 00:03:19.280
- You're joking.
- Nope.

33
00:03:19.282 --> 00:03:21.670
- Really?
- Yeah, really.

34
00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:23.084
Curse the child!

35
00:03:24.587 --> 00:03:26.404
- Yeah.
- Wow, thank you.

36
00:03:29.225 --> 00:03:30.509
Thank you.

37
00:05:02.848 --> 00:05:03.953
You all right?

38
00:05:03.954 --> 00:05:06.147
Yeah.

39
00:05:08.824 --> 00:05:10.745
All right, come on.

40
00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:15.231
- Dr. Talzani.
- Mr. Walsh.

41
00:05:15.233 --> 00:05:17.519
- How are you?
- Busy. Go.

42
00:05:18.871 --> 00:05:20.098
David!

43
00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:31.474
Jesus.

44
00:05:32.482 --> 00:05:35.051
- The smell?
- Yeah.

45
00:05:35.052 --> 00:05:37.518
I thought you guys
are used to this kind of thing.

46
00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:38.974
How can anyone get used to this?

47
00:05:39.557 --> 00:05:42.992
Well, considering we have
no ventilation, no running water,

48
00:05:42.993 --> 00:05:45.494
and no medicine,
I'd say we are doing pretty good.

49
00:05:45.496 --> 00:05:47.731
Dr. Talzani, this is my friend David.

50
00:05:47.732 --> 00:05:50.032
Nice to meet you.

51
00:05:50.034 --> 00:05:52.435
- All right, you know the rules.
- No flash.

52
00:05:52.436 --> 00:05:54.888
Keep out of your way.

53
00:08:08.304 --> 00:08:11.626
- What happened to you?
- Just needed some fresh air.

54
00:08:13.211 --> 00:08:15.531
- Feeling better now?
- Yeah.

55
00:08:15.912 --> 00:08:18.981
All right. You do look like shit.

56
00:08:18.983 --> 00:08:20.750
I think you're getting too old
for this gig.

57
00:08:20.751 --> 00:08:22.035
Now, that's funny.

58
00:08:23.188 --> 00:08:25.754
I'm going home, Mark.

59
00:08:25.755 --> 00:08:28.458
Yeah, me too. Soon.

60
00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:29.686
I want to go now.

61
00:08:31.096 --> 00:08:32.255
Tomorrow.

62
00:08:32.663 --> 00:08:34.698
I've had enough.
And Diane's due in two weeks.

63
00:08:34.699 --> 00:08:35.999
If I'm not back in time...

64
00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:39.103
I know.
What about the offensive?

65
00:08:39.104 --> 00:08:40.270
What about it?

66
00:08:40.271 --> 00:08:44.609
Well, it looks like
they're finally gonna go.

67
00:08:44.611 --> 00:08:47.544
They've been saying that
ever since we got here.

68
00:08:47.545 --> 00:08:50.948
These people have been launching
offensives for, what, two centuries?

69
00:08:50.949 --> 00:08:53.950
Two centuries from now,
they'll still be launching them.

70
00:08:53.951 --> 00:08:56.753
We miss this one,
we catch the next.

71
00:08:56.755 --> 00:08:59.538
That simple, huh?

72
00:09:01.057 --> 00:09:02.411
Ah, shite, here we go.

73
00:09:03.128 --> 00:09:05.322
Blue tags.

74
00:09:07.198 --> 00:09:09.518
- You all right for this?
- Mm, yep.

75
00:09:10.236 --> 00:09:12.768
All right.
Let's go get some shots.

76
00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:14.396
Come on.

77
00:10:30.650 --> 00:10:33.400
Do you have an extra cigarette?

78
00:10:36.788 --> 00:10:38.855
Beautiful, isn't it?

79
00:10:38.856 --> 00:10:40.960
This place is beautiful.

80
00:10:40.961 --> 00:10:43.279
You know...

81
00:10:45.597 --> 00:10:51.003
In my lifetime alone,
we had eight wars.

82
00:10:51.005 --> 00:10:53.337
Two with the Turks,
three with Iranians,

83
00:10:53.338 --> 00:10:56.375
and three with Iraqis.

84
00:10:56.376 --> 00:10:59.312
And if I go back
to when my father was alive,

85
00:10:59.313 --> 00:11:01.279
or my father's father,

86
00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:03.146
each time we've been beaten.

87
00:11:03.148 --> 00:11:05.884
That's what we Kurds do best,
get beaten.

88
00:11:05.886 --> 00:11:07.304
So why do you stay?

89
00:11:08.321 --> 00:11:10.309
Where am I to go, Mr. Walsh?

90
00:11:12.258 --> 00:11:13.657
They talk of free will,

91
00:11:13.659 --> 00:11:17.650
but we are all just homing pigeons
in the end.

92
00:11:19.532 --> 00:11:22.767
You should leave soon,
you and your friend.

93
00:11:22.768 --> 00:11:24.535
The offensive starts in two days,

94
00:11:24.537 --> 00:11:27.104
and when it happens,
it will be very hard to leave.

95
00:11:27.106 --> 00:11:30.108
Well, we don't want to leave.

96
00:11:30.109 --> 00:11:33.412
That's why we're here, Doctor.

97
00:11:33.413 --> 00:11:35.105
Can you get us a place on board?

98
00:11:35.748 --> 00:11:37.533
Do you ever stop?

99
00:11:42.988 --> 00:11:45.654
I must go.
I'll see if I can do something.

100
00:11:45.657 --> 00:11:46.683
Thank you.

101
00:11:56.134 --> 00:11:57.736
There's an Iraqi outpost there,

102
00:11:57.738 --> 00:12:00.038
and the only way to re-supply it
is by convoy through this valley.

103
00:12:00.039 --> 00:12:01.872
So they'll be sitting ducks
for an ambush.

104
00:12:01.874 --> 00:12:03.640
Talzani found us two places
on the truck.

105
00:12:03.642 --> 00:12:05.010
- Has he?
- Yeah.

106
00:12:05.012 --> 00:12:06.844
So we go along for that,
and then come back here,

107
00:12:06.845 --> 00:12:09.512
jump in a Jeep to the border,
we'll be in Turkey by that night.

108
00:12:09.514 --> 00:12:12.450
- What do you think?
- That's cutting it too close.

109
00:12:12.451 --> 00:12:15.151
How? We'll be home by Tuesday,
Wednesday night at the latest.

110
00:12:15.153 --> 00:12:17.986
That's a week and a half
before the baby's due.

111
00:12:17.989 --> 00:12:20.326
Oh, come on, Mark,
what the hell does that mean?

112
00:12:20.327 --> 00:12:22.419
Babies are born early all the time.

113
00:12:26.332 --> 00:12:29.701
- So what if the Iraqis hit back hard?
- That's not gonna happen.

114
00:12:29.703 --> 00:12:31.002
It'll be days, it'll be weeks

115
00:12:31.003 --> 00:12:33.639
probably before the Iraqis
come back into the mountains.

116
00:12:33.641 --> 00:12:35.105
How do you know that?

117
00:12:35.107 --> 00:12:37.609
Because Saddam doesn't give
a fuck about the Kurds.

118
00:12:37.611 --> 00:12:40.045
They'll come back into the mountains
just to prove a point.

119
00:12:40.047 --> 00:12:41.081
Massacre them.

120
00:12:41.082 --> 00:12:43.516
He doesn't fucking care about them,
though he cares about the flats.

121
00:12:43.517 --> 00:12:44.582
Oil fields.

122
00:12:44.583 --> 00:12:46.619
First order of business,
reclaim the oil fields...

123
00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:49.621
Reclaim the oil fields? The Kurd's
haven't even taken them yet.

124
00:12:49.622 --> 00:12:52.125
What if they don't? What then?

125
00:12:52.127 --> 00:12:53.760
Jesus Christ, man,
you're making it hard.

126
00:12:53.762 --> 00:12:55.964
Look, we've been here
what, nearly a month at this stage?

127
00:12:55.965 --> 00:12:57.662
Yeah.

128
00:12:57.664 --> 00:12:59.832
And what have we got? Some shots
of Dr. Talzani and the triage.

129
00:12:59.833 --> 00:13:01.967
We've got some schoolchildren
saluting the rebel flag.

130
00:13:01.968 --> 00:13:03.836
Got some fucking
Pesh Merga running drills?

131
00:13:03.838 --> 00:13:04.803
It's all good stuff.

132
00:13:04.805 --> 00:13:07.204
It's all good stuff, man,
but it's not why I came here.

133
00:13:07.205 --> 00:13:09.594
We haven't got the story.

134
00:13:10.910 --> 00:13:12.477
Look, if this offensive goes off,

135
00:13:12.479 --> 00:13:13.981
Kurdistan will be
hitting the headlines

136
00:13:13.982 --> 00:13:15.149
just about the time we get home.

137
00:13:15.151 --> 00:13:16.616
And we're the only
two photographers here.

138
00:13:16.617 --> 00:13:19.278
There's no reason
why we don't get those shots.

139
00:13:20.856 --> 00:13:23.811
Fucking hell.

140
00:13:24.225 --> 00:13:26.613
I can see it now.

141
00:13:27.295 --> 00:13:29.059
Boys, welcome back.
How was it?

142
00:13:29.062 --> 00:13:30.197
It was grand!

143
00:13:30.198 --> 00:13:31.796
So what shots have you got?

144
00:13:31.798 --> 00:13:33.635
Shots of what, the offensive?
Fuck the offensive.

145
00:13:33.637 --> 00:13:35.400
Look, we've got schoolchildren.
They're very cute.

146
00:13:35.401 --> 00:13:36.822
Okay, Mark, I get the picture.

147
00:13:39.839 --> 00:13:41.623
Two more days, man.

148
00:13:42.909 --> 00:13:45.345
- You know what the worst of this is?
- What?

149
00:13:45.347 --> 00:13:49.583
The food. The fucking food.
I mean, take a look at these beans.

150
00:13:49.584 --> 00:13:51.818
Why do war zones
always have such miserable food?

151
00:13:51.819 --> 00:13:54.153
And you being a Scotsman,
you'd know about cuisine, would you?

152
00:13:54.155 --> 00:13:55.123
Oh, here we go.

153
00:13:55.125 --> 00:13:58.390
Your entire menu looks like
it was made in a fucking war zone.

154
00:13:58.392 --> 00:13:59.659
And by the way,
that's what your kid's...

155
00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:00.925
gonna be eating
for the rest of his life...

156
00:14:00.927 --> 00:14:02.360
if we don't bring back
some decent pictures.

157
00:14:02.361 --> 00:14:04.496
You're worse than a pit bull.
Do you know that?

158
00:14:04.498 --> 00:14:06.965
Has anyone ever managed
to say no to you?

159
00:14:06.967 --> 00:14:09.267
Let's say we hope
for a war in France.

160
00:14:09.268 --> 00:14:11.202
Nice food, close to home.

161
00:14:11.205 --> 00:14:13.900
The only thing is, knowing the French
it'd be over in less than a week.

162
00:14:14.374 --> 00:14:16.794
After this, I switch
to fashion photography.

163
00:14:21.847 --> 00:14:24.350
Two more days, then.
But no more.

164
00:14:24.352 --> 00:14:26.151
And if they come back tomorrow
and say "Just another"...

165
00:14:26.153 --> 00:14:28.988
Then we go. No questions asked.
Absolutely.

166
00:14:28.989 --> 00:14:30.122
Okay.

167
00:14:30.125 --> 00:14:32.375
Good man, fair play.

168
00:14:36.663 --> 00:14:40.588
I cannot believe
I've been talked into it again.

169
00:15:14.703 --> 00:15:16.053
Mark!

170
00:15:17.538 --> 00:15:19.390
Where the fuck are you going?

171
00:15:29.217 --> 00:15:32.071
Cease fire!

172
00:15:37.760 --> 00:15:38.793
What's happened?

173
00:15:38.795 --> 00:15:40.193
- We go!
- Go base?

174
00:15:40.195 --> 00:15:41.696
No base.
No, we're going Iraq.

175
00:15:41.698 --> 00:15:44.516
- Iraqi fort?
- Yes. We go.

176
00:15:45.031 --> 00:15:46.988
You all right?

177
00:15:48.103 --> 00:15:50.796
What was all that about?!

178
00:15:52.408 --> 00:15:55.075
I can't do this anymore, man.

179
00:15:55.077 --> 00:15:56.310
There's been a change in plans.

180
00:15:56.311 --> 00:15:58.442
They're not going back to base,
they're going to hit the Iraqi fort.

181
00:15:58.443 --> 00:15:59.416
What?

182
00:15:59.418 --> 00:16:00.546
They're gonna go up there
in the truck.

183
00:16:00.547 --> 00:16:02.516
- The Iraqis'll think it's a convoy...
- What, we going? No, no, no.

184
00:16:02.517 --> 00:16:05.284
Look, David! We've no choice, man!
We're along for the ride, remember?

185
00:16:05.286 --> 00:16:08.688
Along for the fucking ride?!
What are you saying?

186
00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:10.758
Listen to yourself!

187
00:16:10.759 --> 00:16:13.593
It's another day, then another day,
then another day,

188
00:16:13.595 --> 00:16:15.152
and I can't do it!

189
00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:21.953
I'm going home, man!

190
00:16:23.937 --> 00:16:25.404
David.

191
00:16:25.406 --> 00:16:27.340
What the fuck are you gonna do,
walk home?!

192
00:16:27.342 --> 00:16:30.969
It's 20 fucking miles back to base!

193
00:16:31.546 --> 00:16:33.180
Just don't get it, do you?

194
00:16:33.182 --> 00:16:35.342
I don't care.

195
00:16:36.484 --> 00:16:38.575
- David!
- I'm going home.

196
00:16:40.388 --> 00:16:42.054
Fuck! David!

197
00:16:42.056 --> 00:16:45.048
Mr. Walsh, are you coming?

198
00:17:41.517 --> 00:17:43.573
Mr. Walsh?

199
00:17:45.621 --> 00:17:49.716
They found you down by the river.
Do you remember that?

200
00:17:57.399 --> 00:18:00.559
He says you can't walk.
Is that so?

201
00:18:01.504 --> 00:18:05.757
Not sure. I, um, just woke up.

202
00:18:06.341 --> 00:18:08.194
Does it hurt?

203
00:18:09.813 --> 00:18:13.099
- So you can feel it?
- Yeah.

204
00:18:17.052 --> 00:18:18.110
No pain?

205
00:18:20.424 --> 00:18:22.481
Can you move your arms?

206
00:18:24.093 --> 00:18:25.512
Your legs?

207
00:18:28.598 --> 00:18:29.621
Very good.

208
00:18:33.768 --> 00:18:35.931
A head wound as well?

209
00:18:39.374 --> 00:18:41.375
Flesh wound.

210
00:18:41.377 --> 00:18:45.048
Maybe concussion,
but no skull fracture.

211
00:18:45.049 --> 00:18:49.118
Kurdistan is not a good place
for skull fracture.

212
00:18:49.119 --> 00:18:50.812
Well...

213
00:18:53.089 --> 00:18:57.326
You took quite a jolt,
but you're not paralyzed.

214
00:18:57.327 --> 00:19:00.696
And it seems
there are no broken bones.

215
00:19:00.697 --> 00:19:03.831
Legs will be the biggest problem.

216
00:19:03.833 --> 00:19:07.858
That's always the case.
Legs, legs, legs...

217
00:19:08.272 --> 00:19:10.806
For every arm
I've amputated up here,

218
00:19:10.808 --> 00:19:13.576
I've probably taken 10 legs.

219
00:19:13.578 --> 00:19:15.778
Strange, isn't it?

220
00:19:15.779 --> 00:19:20.477
Human legs are just not designed
for modern war.

221
00:19:26.990 --> 00:19:30.559
Take it easy. Get some rest.

222
00:19:30.561 --> 00:19:35.554
You have some neural disruption
but, Inshallah, it's only temporary.

223
00:19:37.334 --> 00:19:40.026
You're going to be right.

224
00:20:39.530 --> 00:20:41.816
How do you feel?

225
00:20:42.200 --> 00:20:43.288
Geez.

226
00:20:45.068 --> 00:20:47.456
Scared me.

227
00:20:49.639 --> 00:20:50.640
Better.

228
00:20:50.641 --> 00:20:52.975
Only a little bit of pain.

229
00:20:52.977 --> 00:20:56.812
Excellent. Pain is always
preferable to numbness.

230
00:20:56.814 --> 00:20:59.681
You were lucky, Mr. Walsh.
Really lucky.

231
00:20:59.683 --> 00:21:02.050
If there'd been any complications...

232
00:21:02.052 --> 00:21:03.439
What?

233
00:21:04.286 --> 00:21:05.687
You would have shot me?

234
00:21:05.689 --> 00:21:11.352
Yeah. For your own good.
To stop you from suffering.

235
00:21:12.461 --> 00:21:16.567
And what about your friend?
He never came to visit you.

236
00:21:16.568 --> 00:21:20.024
He wouldn't even know I'm here.

237
00:21:21.173 --> 00:21:24.806
He left the day before it happened.

238
00:21:24.807 --> 00:21:27.844
First day of the offensive.

239
00:21:27.845 --> 00:21:29.580
Smart man.

240
00:21:29.582 --> 00:21:33.749
You war people,
you all think you're immortal.

241
00:21:33.751 --> 00:21:35.852
Probably not good for morale

242
00:21:35.853 --> 00:21:38.547
to see a colleague
end up in Harir cave.

243
00:21:40.292 --> 00:21:43.024
That's all I can find.

244
00:21:43.026 --> 00:21:44.482
I hope they fit.

245
00:22:54.065 --> 00:22:59.193
Oh, Mr. Walsh.
You're making progress.

246
00:23:03.007 --> 00:23:04.840
What do you think?

247
00:23:04.842 --> 00:23:07.811
How many doctors in the world
have to clean their gun?

248
00:23:07.812 --> 00:23:10.146
You should sterilize it, too.

249
00:23:10.147 --> 00:23:12.816
Oh, sarcasm is back.

250
00:23:12.818 --> 00:23:14.739
Great.

251
00:23:17.255 --> 00:23:19.416
You must have a lot of regret.

252
00:23:23.527 --> 00:23:25.881
Regret what, Mr. Walsh?

253
00:23:27.297 --> 00:23:30.535
That I spared them
a few days of terrible suffering

254
00:23:30.536 --> 00:23:36.239
'cause I have no way of saving them
or easing the unbearable pain?

255
00:23:36.241 --> 00:23:40.437
Do you think I kill those poor men
because I enjoy it?

256
00:23:41.278 --> 00:23:44.235
Geez, no.

257
00:23:49.989 --> 00:23:53.058
In quiet times,

258
00:23:53.060 --> 00:23:58.096
when there is not a lot of fighting
and I have more time,

259
00:23:58.097 --> 00:24:00.298
I give out very few blues.

260
00:24:00.299 --> 00:24:05.836
But when there is a lot of fighting,
a lot of wounded coming in...

261
00:24:05.838 --> 00:24:07.931
No.

262
00:24:08.540 --> 00:24:12.359
I have to think of those
I can still save.

263
00:24:21.387 --> 00:24:23.171
That's the one.

264
00:24:25.755 --> 00:24:29.762
Some live, some die,
that's the only way to look at it.

265
00:24:29.763 --> 00:24:31.682
Thank you.

266
00:24:32.097 --> 00:24:35.232
Anything else is just arrogance,

267
00:24:35.233 --> 00:24:40.591
the arrogance of imagining
you can do something to change it.

268
00:24:45.445 --> 00:24:48.446
I'm sure they lecture against this
in medical school,

269
00:24:48.447 --> 00:24:50.913
smoking in operating room.

270
00:24:50.915 --> 00:24:53.007
I think you'll get away
with it tonight.

271
00:24:53.652 --> 00:24:56.678
Do you know
what Pesh Merga means?

272
00:24:58.024 --> 00:25:01.152
It means, "those who face death".

273
00:25:02.562 --> 00:25:04.812
Romantic.

274
00:25:06.297 --> 00:25:08.698
Poetic, even.

275
00:25:08.699 --> 00:25:11.736
Personally, I've never seen
one face death.

276
00:25:11.737 --> 00:25:14.990
They all turn away at the end.

277
00:25:23.083 --> 00:25:26.452
A Jeep is leaving tomorrow morning
for the frontier.

278
00:25:26.453 --> 00:25:29.853
- Why?
- The offensive has been discussed.

279
00:25:29.855 --> 00:25:32.675
You better leave while you can.

280
00:25:46.338 --> 00:25:48.658
Set to go?

281
00:25:50.211 --> 00:25:51.410
Yep.

282
00:25:51.411 --> 00:25:55.038
Ah, it's much better.

283
00:25:55.615 --> 00:25:57.397
I guess it's yours.

284
00:25:58.251 --> 00:26:00.570
You're joking me.

285
00:26:02.755 --> 00:26:05.005
I hope you had an enjoyable visit.

286
00:26:05.457 --> 00:26:07.757
A lovely trip.

287
00:26:07.759 --> 00:26:10.683
Don't know why you don't get
more tourists over in these parts.

288
00:26:11.963 --> 00:26:14.967
I'm sorry. It's very Kurdish.

289
00:26:14.968 --> 00:26:18.369
It's a point of honor
to make sure strangers are content.

290
00:26:18.371 --> 00:26:21.396
Well, in that case, just think of me
as another happy customer.

291
00:26:22.441 --> 00:26:24.676
Dr. Talzani.

292
00:26:24.677 --> 00:26:26.977
Thank you so much.

293
00:26:26.979 --> 00:26:28.445
Pleasure is mine.

294
00:26:28.446 --> 00:26:31.869
Mr. Walsh, good luck.

295
00:28:25.198 --> 00:28:26.465
Oh!

296
00:28:26.466 --> 00:28:27.695
Oh!

297
00:28:30.770 --> 00:28:32.603
No, whoa, whoa, whoa.

298
00:28:32.605 --> 00:28:34.357
Easy, easy, easy.

299
00:28:37.410 --> 00:28:39.979
Oh! Dios mio!
What happened to you?

300
00:28:39.981 --> 00:28:41.012
Oh, it was dark.

301
00:28:41.013 --> 00:28:45.919
I fell into a river and got dragged
over some rocks a bit, banged up.

302
00:28:45.920 --> 00:28:47.587
Just another day at the office.

303
00:28:47.589 --> 00:28:49.989
Why didn't you call me
to say you're coming?

304
00:28:49.990 --> 00:28:53.481
'Cause I had a quick connection
from Istanbul.

305
00:28:56.098 --> 00:28:59.432
Well, what's this?
What do we got here?

306
00:28:59.433 --> 00:29:00.367
You like it?

307
00:29:00.369 --> 00:29:01.934
Yeah, I love it
when you go shopping

308
00:29:01.936 --> 00:29:04.305
and then, all of a sudden, presto!

309
00:29:04.306 --> 00:29:08.107
There's something there
that makes the place less... simple.

310
00:29:08.109 --> 00:29:10.074
You mean monastic?

311
00:29:10.075 --> 00:29:11.510
No, simple.

312
00:29:11.511 --> 00:29:14.068
We had nowhere to sit.

313
00:29:15.081 --> 00:29:16.647
- I'll take it back if you want.
- No, no.

314
00:29:16.649 --> 00:29:19.185
Jesus, baby. I'm joking, I'm joking.
I love it.

315
00:29:19.187 --> 00:29:21.311
Well, I don't love it,
but I'll get used to it.

316
00:29:22.190 --> 00:29:24.156
I'm just tired.

317
00:29:24.157 --> 00:29:26.023
Come on, I'll throw you a bath.

318
00:29:26.025 --> 00:29:27.948
Okay.

319
00:29:28.594 --> 00:29:32.314
By the way,
I love your new style.

320
00:29:38.573 --> 00:29:40.423
Hey.

321
00:30:10.740 --> 00:30:13.761
Oh, my God.

322
00:30:21.049 --> 00:30:26.854
Mark, stand up.
Let me dry you off.

323
00:30:26.857 --> 00:30:29.241
Come on, baby.

324
00:30:30.992 --> 00:30:32.980
Missed you.

325
00:30:56.886 --> 00:31:00.255
Morning, sunshine. I don't think
I've ever been that tired.

326
00:31:00.256 --> 00:31:03.005
I can't even remember
getting out of the bath.

327
00:31:07.929 --> 00:31:12.901
Mi amor. What happened to you?
You're all black and blue.

328
00:31:12.902 --> 00:31:14.568
I told you. I'm an idiot.

329
00:31:14.570 --> 00:31:16.670
Fell into a river
and got dragged over some rocks.

330
00:31:16.671 --> 00:31:19.058
You got all that from some rocks?

331
00:31:19.643 --> 00:31:22.769
It's a strong river, love.

332
00:31:26.915 --> 00:31:30.184
Diane called.
David didn't come back with you?

333
00:31:30.186 --> 00:31:34.020
No. We split up the first day
of the offensive.

334
00:31:34.022 --> 00:31:35.980
He wanted to get back.

335
00:31:36.724 --> 00:31:38.459
What, do you mean
he's not back yet?

336
00:31:38.460 --> 00:31:39.726
No.

337
00:31:39.728 --> 00:31:41.861
That doesn't make sense.

338
00:31:41.862 --> 00:31:45.466
He should've been back
four or five days ago.

339
00:31:45.468 --> 00:31:47.890
Should we be worried?

340
00:31:48.871 --> 00:31:50.671
No.

341
00:31:50.673 --> 00:31:53.072
No. He probably just got cut off
by the Iraqis.

342
00:31:53.073 --> 00:31:57.178
- They came back in fairly hard.
- Well, that's pretty worrisome.

343
00:31:57.180 --> 00:31:58.845
David knows what to do.

344
00:31:58.846 --> 00:32:02.614
He'll just lay low for a while,
find another way out.

345
00:32:02.616 --> 00:32:04.752
You know Diane is due
in a week or so.

346
00:32:04.753 --> 00:32:07.555
- What if David isn't back by then?
- He'll be back by then.

347
00:32:07.557 --> 00:32:10.057
He wouldn't miss it for the world.
It's all he talked about over there.

348
00:32:10.059 --> 00:32:13.549
- Yeah, but imagine what if he isn't?
- I don't know.

349
00:32:16.999 --> 00:32:20.300
What, do you want me
to go back over and find him?

350
00:32:20.301 --> 00:32:21.619
Is that what you want?

351
00:32:22.404 --> 00:32:24.268
No.

352
00:32:24.270 --> 00:32:27.594
No, carino. Of course not.

353
00:32:27.843 --> 00:32:30.002
I just...

354
00:32:30.847 --> 00:32:33.815
I just wonder sometimes
if you guys even think about us

355
00:32:33.816 --> 00:32:35.549
when you're out there?

356
00:32:35.551 --> 00:32:37.352
That's all.

357
00:32:37.354 --> 00:32:38.787
I think about you on the way out,

358
00:32:38.788 --> 00:32:41.087
and I think about you
on the way back.

359
00:32:41.089 --> 00:32:43.259
I try not to think about you
out there.

360
00:32:43.260 --> 00:32:44.713
Just makes it too hard.

361
00:32:50.299 --> 00:32:52.117
Easy.

362
00:32:52.868 --> 00:32:56.003
You're killing me with these hugs.

363
00:32:56.004 --> 00:32:58.073
I have to go to work.

364
00:32:58.074 --> 00:33:00.141
- You have to?
- Yeah.

365
00:33:00.142 --> 00:33:01.800
You know I do, honey.

366
00:33:02.646 --> 00:33:06.137
But I'll try to come back early.

367
00:33:59.936 --> 00:34:02.772
- Hey, hey.
- Mark!

368
00:34:02.774 --> 00:34:04.540
How are you, Ivan?

369
00:34:04.541 --> 00:34:06.106
Good, good.

370
00:34:06.108 --> 00:34:08.576
I was growing worried with all
this news of the fighting out there.

371
00:34:08.577 --> 00:34:09.706
Oh, yeah?

372
00:34:09.747 --> 00:34:11.047
What happened to you?

373
00:34:11.048 --> 00:34:14.903
Nothing. Got into a bind
and fell into a river is all.

374
00:34:15.584 --> 00:34:17.687
You go to Kurdistan
to fall into a river?

375
00:34:17.688 --> 00:34:20.489
Yeah, well, no one ever said
I was smart.

376
00:34:20.491 --> 00:34:22.226
Listen, can you do
a rush job on these?

377
00:34:22.227 --> 00:34:24.459
I wanna take advantage
of it being in the news, you know?

378
00:34:24.461 --> 00:34:26.160
How much of a rush?

379
00:34:26.162 --> 00:34:28.414
This afternoon?

380
00:34:29.500 --> 00:34:30.624
All right.

381
00:34:31.136 --> 00:34:33.169
I'll make you copies
to show on the wall, okay?

382
00:34:33.170 --> 00:34:34.796
Good man.

383
00:34:40.110 --> 00:34:41.309
You know it?

384
00:34:41.310 --> 00:34:43.904
Yeah, it's a great shot.

385
00:34:44.715 --> 00:34:48.016
How long have you been doing
this now, the war stuff?

386
00:34:48.017 --> 00:34:50.246
- Eight, nine years?
- Twelve.

387
00:34:51.522 --> 00:34:53.824
You should get out.

388
00:34:53.825 --> 00:34:56.760
I can tell by looking at you
that it's time to get out.

389
00:34:56.761 --> 00:34:58.392
You know by looking at me?

390
00:34:58.394 --> 00:35:01.129
Well, I've been there, you know?

391
00:35:01.130 --> 00:35:03.963
And I know that, for all of us,
there comes the time to, well,

392
00:35:03.966 --> 00:35:05.167
to leave it, you know?

393
00:35:05.169 --> 00:35:07.869
Yeah, well, that happens
out there, man.

394
00:35:07.871 --> 00:35:10.636
I deal with it out there.

395
00:35:10.638 --> 00:35:13.577
And then, I'm here.
Separate worlds.

396
00:35:13.578 --> 00:35:15.168
You know, separate.

397
00:35:16.915 --> 00:35:18.380
Why, you don't believe me?

398
00:35:18.382 --> 00:35:19.902
No.

399
00:35:20.350 --> 00:35:22.218
It doesn't affect me, Ivan.

400
00:35:22.220 --> 00:35:24.344
Look me in the eye and tell me that.

401
00:35:25.589 --> 00:35:27.057
It doesn't affect me.

402
00:35:27.058 --> 00:35:29.992
Well, that's the saddest thing
I've ever heard, son.

403
00:35:29.994 --> 00:35:31.792
Ivan, I just came in
to get the fucking films done.

404
00:35:31.794 --> 00:35:33.330
I didn't come in for a lecture, man.

405
00:35:33.331 --> 00:35:34.764
All right, all right.
I'm sorry, I'm sorry.

406
00:35:34.766 --> 00:35:35.831
It's none of my business, I know.

407
00:35:35.832 --> 00:35:37.332
I just think of you as a friend,
that's all.

408
00:35:37.334 --> 00:35:38.309
Fair enough.

409
00:35:38.310 --> 00:35:39.636
Okay, so, 5 o'clock?

410
00:35:39.637 --> 00:35:41.803
Yeah. Bill me. Thanks.

411
00:35:41.805 --> 00:35:43.534
Okay.

412
00:35:52.181 --> 00:35:54.775
Give us a pint, Kevin, will ya?
Please.

413
00:36:13.870 --> 00:36:15.930
Cheers, Kevin.

414
00:37:58.076 --> 00:38:00.111
Hey. Stretching.

415
00:38:00.112 --> 00:38:01.945
You've already started
going through them.

416
00:38:01.947 --> 00:38:03.414
- Yeah.
- How are they?

417
00:38:03.416 --> 00:38:05.735
- Good. Got some good ones.
- Show me.

418
00:38:05.983 --> 00:38:09.643
No, not now.
I'll show you later...

419
00:38:10.054 --> 00:38:12.144
Missy.

420
00:38:13.157 --> 00:38:16.011
Hey, I missed you today.

421
00:38:16.459 --> 00:38:19.313
I missed you, too.

422
00:38:26.470 --> 00:38:28.595
There you are.

423
00:39:15.053 --> 00:39:16.211
David!

424
00:39:22.529 --> 00:39:24.516
Elena?

425
00:39:31.737 --> 00:39:33.864
I'm sorry I'm late.

426
00:39:34.437 --> 00:39:37.190
I had a starter.
I had to feed the beast.

427
00:39:37.976 --> 00:39:39.978
If I don't eat on time,
it gets really angry.

428
00:39:39.979 --> 00:39:42.639
It moved! Did you feel it?

429
00:39:42.882 --> 00:39:45.043
Of course you did.

430
00:39:46.987 --> 00:39:48.119
How are you?

431
00:39:48.120 --> 00:39:49.722
Yeah, all right.

432
00:39:49.724 --> 00:39:51.690
Except I have to pee
every five minutes.

433
00:39:51.691 --> 00:39:54.282
Any word from David, hmm?

434
00:39:55.528 --> 00:39:57.597
I know it's his job,

435
00:39:57.598 --> 00:39:59.732
and there's a war on,
and it's a primitive place,

436
00:39:59.733 --> 00:40:02.499
but surely he could get
a message out to me somehow.

437
00:40:02.501 --> 00:40:06.005
Or maybe not.
If the phone lines have been cut...

438
00:40:06.006 --> 00:40:08.638
Well, forget a message.
I want him.

439
00:40:08.640 --> 00:40:12.109
He does this to me
and then fucks off to Kurdistan.

440
00:40:12.111 --> 00:40:13.860
Yeah.

441
00:40:14.747 --> 00:40:19.884
Hey, what if I send a cable
to the Kurdish refugee camp?

442
00:40:19.886 --> 00:40:21.452
I know some people working there.

443
00:40:21.453 --> 00:40:24.188
I can have them
at least ask around, no?

444
00:40:24.190 --> 00:40:26.458
Oh, I don't know.

445
00:40:26.459 --> 00:40:28.759
The idea of some Red Cross guy
running up to him,

446
00:40:28.761 --> 00:40:31.696
telling him he has to call his wife,
he'd get pissed off.

447
00:40:31.697 --> 00:40:32.948
So what?

448
00:40:34.200 --> 00:40:36.759
You know what, you're right.

449
00:40:37.235 --> 00:40:40.795
Okay. Let's do it.

450
00:40:56.790 --> 00:40:58.723
Mark!

451
00:40:58.725 --> 00:41:00.976
- How are you?
- Good.

452
00:41:03.362 --> 00:41:05.029
You look like shite!

453
00:41:05.031 --> 00:41:06.298
Thanks.

454
00:41:06.299 --> 00:41:09.393
- What happened?
- Oh, you know.

455
00:41:09.837 --> 00:41:11.770
So, good trip?

456
00:41:11.772 --> 00:41:14.238
I think so, yeah. You tell me.

457
00:41:14.239 --> 00:41:15.193
Give!

458
00:41:15.476 --> 00:41:20.380
It's a Pesh Merga ambush
of an Iraqi convoy.

459
00:41:20.381 --> 00:41:24.383
They killed 19 troops
and took out a transportation truck.

460
00:41:24.384 --> 00:41:26.249
Yeah.

461
00:41:26.251 --> 00:41:29.021
Too bad Life magazine
only does pap these days,

462
00:41:29.023 --> 00:41:32.591
but I'll run these past them anyway.

463
00:41:32.592 --> 00:41:36.062
We should be able to find
something on the continent.

464
00:41:36.064 --> 00:41:38.066
Hear there's a lot of Kurds
in Germany, right?

465
00:41:38.067 --> 00:41:39.132
Or do I mean Turks?

466
00:41:39.133 --> 00:41:41.632
You mean both, Amy.
Kurds and Turks.

467
00:41:41.634 --> 00:41:45.103
Yeah, okay. I'll try Stern.

468
00:41:45.104 --> 00:41:46.797
What else?

469
00:41:47.672 --> 00:41:49.008
"Triage".

470
00:41:49.009 --> 00:41:50.242
Working title.

471
00:41:50.243 --> 00:41:52.513
- For what?
- Um...

472
00:41:52.515 --> 00:41:54.880
Makeshift hospital in the field.

473
00:41:54.881 --> 00:41:57.149
Soldiers that came in
that were too gravely wounded,

474
00:41:57.151 --> 00:41:59.879
they took them outside
and killed them.

475
00:42:00.220 --> 00:42:01.586
Killed?

476
00:42:01.587 --> 00:42:02.593
By?

477
00:42:02.594 --> 00:42:05.377
Their own doctor.
Fellow Kurd.

478
00:42:06.393 --> 00:42:09.059
You can see him in the shots.

479
00:42:09.061 --> 00:42:10.594
Jesus Christ!

480
00:42:10.595 --> 00:42:13.121
Mercy killings.

481
00:42:13.801 --> 00:42:20.372
Well, that is an incredible
piece of work, Mark.

482
00:42:20.373 --> 00:42:22.643
Could put these up for auction.

483
00:42:22.645 --> 00:42:24.179
I'll send it to all the news weeklies,

484
00:42:24.180 --> 00:42:27.044
and I'll tell them I need an answer
by tomorrow afternoon.

485
00:42:27.047 --> 00:42:28.513
Nice.

486
00:42:28.515 --> 00:42:31.684
Listen, Time magazine
is sending a reporter to Burma,

487
00:42:31.685 --> 00:42:35.022
and they need a photographer.

488
00:42:35.023 --> 00:42:37.146
When they see these...

489
00:42:37.923 --> 00:42:39.192
Interested?

490
00:42:39.194 --> 00:42:40.714
Time? Yeah, sure.

491
00:42:41.194 --> 00:42:43.128
Well, don't jump for joy, will you?

492
00:42:43.130 --> 00:42:44.755
God.

493
00:42:45.266 --> 00:42:47.401
- You all right?
- Yeah, I'm just tired.

494
00:42:47.402 --> 00:42:49.059
It's a long trip.

495
00:42:49.904 --> 00:42:52.405
- Okay, anything else?
- No, that's it.

496
00:42:52.406 --> 00:42:54.073
Okay, I'll call you. Bye!

497
00:42:54.075 --> 00:42:55.632
- See ya, Amy.
- See ya.

498
00:42:57.178 --> 00:42:59.977
That's a terrible limp you got there,
Mark. You sure you're okay?

499
00:42:59.980 --> 00:43:02.214
Yeah, fine. Just got banged up
a bit, you know.

500
00:43:02.215 --> 00:43:05.017
Don't start being all concerned, now.
It doesn't suit ya.

501
00:43:05.019 --> 00:43:06.084
Well, listen,

502
00:43:06.086 --> 00:43:09.587
you may have got banged up,
but I'd say it was worth it.

503
00:43:09.590 --> 00:43:12.909
- Great work.
- Thanks.

504
00:43:59.307 --> 00:44:01.973
Hey. I probably should have
stayed home.

505
00:44:01.975 --> 00:44:04.912
I'm not much in the mood
for socializing.

506
00:44:04.913 --> 00:44:06.846
You should go and talk to Diane.

507
00:44:06.849 --> 00:44:09.048
I will. I will.

508
00:44:09.049 --> 00:44:10.914
I'm gonna say no
to the Burma job.

509
00:44:10.916 --> 00:44:12.486
Gonna have Amy
pull my name from...

510
00:44:12.487 --> 00:44:15.054
Are you serious?

511
00:44:15.057 --> 00:44:17.525
But you've always talked
about working for Time.

512
00:44:17.527 --> 00:44:19.125
I know. Just not right now.

513
00:44:19.126 --> 00:44:22.118
God knows there'll always be
another war.

514
00:44:23.632 --> 00:44:28.521
Mark, did something happen
in Kurdistan, hmm?

515
00:44:34.108 --> 00:44:36.175
Yes.

516
00:44:36.176 --> 00:44:38.678
I decided I want to spend
more time with you,

517
00:44:38.679 --> 00:44:40.533
and I want to have a baby with you.

518
00:44:43.851 --> 00:44:47.242
I'm sorry, but I'm...
I'm worried about you.

519
00:44:47.923 --> 00:44:49.455
- Worried about me?
- Yeah.

520
00:44:49.456 --> 00:44:52.810
Yeah, something's wrong.
You're not yourself.

521
00:44:56.831 --> 00:44:59.024
I'm gonna see Diane.

522
00:46:41.002 --> 00:46:42.937
That's better.

523
00:46:42.939 --> 00:46:45.873
I don't know what I was thinking
with those heels.

524
00:46:45.874 --> 00:46:48.627
- Here you go.
- Thanks.

525
00:46:51.046 --> 00:46:54.780
It's just so weird.

526
00:46:54.782 --> 00:46:58.317
You know, Mark didn't
even mention David.

527
00:46:58.319 --> 00:47:01.173
It's so strange.

528
00:47:01.757 --> 00:47:03.156
I don't know.

529
00:47:03.158 --> 00:47:07.727
He just seems different,
preoccupied.

530
00:47:07.728 --> 00:47:10.630
And his limp is definitely
getting worse.

531
00:47:10.632 --> 00:47:13.759
He says it isn't, but...

532
00:47:14.136 --> 00:47:16.936
And tonight he told me
he wants to have a baby.

533
00:47:16.938 --> 00:47:18.738
- A baby?
- Yeah.

534
00:47:18.740 --> 00:47:21.276
Mark wants to have a baby?

535
00:47:21.277 --> 00:47:24.470
Wow, he really isn't well.

536
00:47:27.351 --> 00:47:30.637
I'm sorry. I'm sorry, Diane.

537
00:47:31.254 --> 00:47:33.572
My God.
You don't need to hear this.

538
00:47:34.289 --> 00:47:37.019
- I'm sorry.
- It's okay.

539
00:47:45.068 --> 00:47:47.455
Strange, isn't it?

540
00:47:49.037 --> 00:47:52.240
They've gone
to so many places together,

541
00:47:52.241 --> 00:47:55.943
they're best friends,
but they're so different.

542
00:47:55.945 --> 00:48:00.265
David seems to always focus
on the beauty, what's hopeful.

543
00:48:01.049 --> 00:48:03.251
While Mark...

544
00:48:03.252 --> 00:48:07.848
Well, you wouldn't want
many of his pictures on the wall.

545
00:49:27.769 --> 00:49:29.589
Mark?

546
00:49:37.448 --> 00:49:39.539
Mark?

547
00:49:45.323 --> 00:49:46.309
Mark?!

548
00:50:11.749 --> 00:50:13.875
Mrs. Walsh?

549
00:50:14.584 --> 00:50:16.218
He's been sent on to neurology.

550
00:50:16.220 --> 00:50:19.757
We cleaned the head wound
and removed a foreign object.

551
00:50:19.759 --> 00:50:22.824
A foreign object?
What do you mean?

552
00:50:22.825 --> 00:50:25.159
I really couldn't say.
That's pathology.

553
00:50:25.160 --> 00:50:28.130
Hey, sorry.

554
00:50:28.131 --> 00:50:30.399
What is wrong with his legs?

555
00:50:30.400 --> 00:50:32.200
That's neurology, I'm afraid.

556
00:50:32.202 --> 00:50:35.204
But I'm sure the neurologist will be
able to answer all your questions.

557
00:50:35.205 --> 00:50:38.342
It should be only
a few more minutes.

558
00:50:48.952 --> 00:50:53.323
So, the good news is
there's nothing permanently wrong.

559
00:50:53.324 --> 00:50:55.658
Nothing physically,
permanently wrong with him,

560
00:50:55.659 --> 00:50:56.758
that is, you know.

561
00:50:56.760 --> 00:51:00.328
No neural disruption,
no broken bones.

562
00:51:00.329 --> 00:51:01.466
There is some swelling

563
00:51:01.467 --> 00:51:03.698
and some hypersensitivity
along the spine.

564
00:51:03.700 --> 00:51:05.734
That's a class symptom
of high-impact trauma,

565
00:51:05.735 --> 00:51:10.140
but all indications are
that it's dissipating.

566
00:51:10.141 --> 00:51:12.275
Then, why is he getting worse?

567
00:51:12.276 --> 00:51:14.008
He told me he fell into a river,

568
00:51:14.010 --> 00:51:15.878
that he was dragged
across some rocks.

569
00:51:15.880 --> 00:51:18.214
- Is that what he told you?
- Yes.

570
00:51:18.217 --> 00:51:20.450
That's when I started to wonder
about his story.

571
00:51:20.451 --> 00:51:24.222
Because we extracted this
from the back of his head.

572
00:51:24.224 --> 00:51:27.122
Um, "composition...
steel and cadmium alloy

573
00:51:27.124 --> 00:51:28.492
with traces of TNT.

574
00:51:28.493 --> 00:51:30.060
Characteristics...
an eighth of an inch thick,

575
00:51:30.062 --> 00:51:31.926
jagged edges,
curled on two sides,

576
00:51:31.928 --> 00:51:35.318
indicating a sudden
and extreme metal-stress".

577
00:51:40.037 --> 00:51:41.457
Shrapnel.

578
00:51:41.905 --> 00:51:45.576
It seems like Mark had
a very close call over there.

579
00:51:45.578 --> 00:51:47.077
An artillery shell exploded

580
00:51:47.078 --> 00:51:49.944
just meters
from where he was standing.

581
00:51:49.946 --> 00:51:51.647
That's how he got this.

582
00:51:51.649 --> 00:51:52.985
But, look, that's not the problem.

583
00:51:52.986 --> 00:51:55.119
By all medical
and physical indicators,

584
00:51:55.120 --> 00:51:56.752
he should be improving.

585
00:51:56.754 --> 00:52:00.655
But he's not. I mean, if anything,
he's getting worse.

586
00:52:00.656 --> 00:52:01.992
Which means?

587
00:52:01.994 --> 00:52:04.494
Which means it's
a psychosomatic complication,

588
00:52:04.495 --> 00:52:05.962
maybe PTSD.

589
00:52:05.964 --> 00:52:07.863
Look, I'm not a psychiatrist,

590
00:52:07.865 --> 00:52:11.961
but I think that might be
the kind of doctor he needs.

591
00:52:13.907 --> 00:52:17.259
Why didn't you tell me
about the explosion?

592
00:52:21.746 --> 00:52:24.737
I thought it would be easier not to.

593
00:52:26.651 --> 00:52:28.552
Easier?

594
00:52:28.554 --> 00:52:30.419
Yeah.

595
00:52:30.421 --> 00:52:32.890
Love, you've already had enough
to worry about.

596
00:52:32.891 --> 00:52:35.517
When I go away, you know?

597
00:52:46.404 --> 00:52:48.840
It's over now.

598
00:52:48.842 --> 00:52:51.034
You're home.

599
00:52:51.743 --> 00:52:53.834
I love you.

600
00:52:55.580 --> 00:52:58.469
Don't be upset at me, Elena.

601
00:53:00.686 --> 00:53:02.915
I'll be fine.

602
00:53:06.090 --> 00:53:09.325
- Everything is going to be okay.
- Yeah.

603
00:53:09.327 --> 00:53:11.453
I'm sure.

604
00:54:10.590 --> 00:54:15.425
... Joaquin Morales.
Leave a message after the beep.

605
00:54:16.495 --> 00:54:19.552
Grandfather, are you there?

606
00:54:20.866 --> 00:54:23.526
It's me, Elena.

607
00:54:25.971 --> 00:54:28.358
Mutilated baby corpse.

608
00:54:28.808 --> 00:54:30.908
Am I right?

609
00:54:30.909 --> 00:54:34.696
In this test, there is
no right or wrong.

610
00:54:35.349 --> 00:54:36.815
Next one.

611
00:54:36.816 --> 00:54:39.248
Oh, Jesus.

612
00:54:39.250 --> 00:54:42.069
Mutilated woman's corpse.

613
00:54:49.127 --> 00:54:52.195
Mutilated man's corpse.
Must be the father.

614
00:54:52.196 --> 00:54:55.326
They got the whole family,
did they, doctor? Fucking hell.

615
00:54:56.168 --> 00:54:58.437
All just a game to you, Mr. Walsh?

616
00:54:58.439 --> 00:55:01.462
I don't know.
It just all seems a bit...

617
00:55:02.142 --> 00:55:04.142
...stupid, you know?

618
00:55:04.144 --> 00:55:06.244
Maybe it is.

619
00:55:06.245 --> 00:55:08.413
But I am trying to help you,

620
00:55:08.414 --> 00:55:11.715
and in 15 minutes,
I'll be leaving here and will get paid

621
00:55:11.716 --> 00:55:13.918
whether you answer
my questions or not,

622
00:55:13.920 --> 00:55:16.188
while you'll still be lying here
unable to walk.

623
00:55:16.189 --> 00:55:17.488
The point being, I suppose,

624
00:55:17.489 --> 00:55:21.207
that stupidity can take
a variety of forms.

625
00:55:22.163 --> 00:55:25.359
Have you ever been
in a war zone, doctor?

626
00:55:25.766 --> 00:55:29.501
No. But I've treated patients
who have.

627
00:55:29.503 --> 00:55:32.236
All right. Well, no offense,
but I don't think it's the same thing.

628
00:55:32.237 --> 00:55:36.229
No, it's not the same thing.

629
00:55:36.908 --> 00:55:39.043
But does that mean
we can only understand

630
00:55:39.044 --> 00:55:42.102
what we directly experience?

631
00:55:42.781 --> 00:55:46.740
Can only someone who's been raped
counsel a rape victim?

632
00:55:48.587 --> 00:55:50.644
Maybe.

633
00:56:03.937 --> 00:56:07.724
I didn't know
Mark had been published.

634
00:56:16.851 --> 00:56:18.785
Do you think you can help him?

635
00:56:18.787 --> 00:56:22.379
Yes, of course I can help him.
I can help anyone.

636
00:56:22.722 --> 00:56:26.590
Like I said last night, it doesn't
change anything between us.

637
00:56:26.592 --> 00:56:29.821
I only asked you here to help him.

638
00:56:31.930 --> 00:56:36.636
How did you become so hard, Elena?

639
00:56:36.637 --> 00:56:39.330
So judgmental?

640
00:56:41.175 --> 00:56:44.631
Is it always wrong
to be judgmental?

641
00:56:45.045 --> 00:56:49.616
About the Nazis
or what Stalin did?

642
00:56:49.618 --> 00:56:51.572
About what you did?

643
00:56:51.986 --> 00:56:57.154
Oh? And just what is it
that you think that I did?

644
00:56:57.156 --> 00:56:58.857
No, no. I'm... I'm sorry.

645
00:56:58.858 --> 00:57:01.458
No, I do want to go into this.

646
00:57:01.460 --> 00:57:05.863
This is something you have
always denied me.

647
00:57:05.864 --> 00:57:08.367
You have learned about what I did,

648
00:57:08.369 --> 00:57:11.770
and that was enough,
you made up your own mind.

649
00:57:11.772 --> 00:57:18.198
But you never once
came to ask me.

650
00:57:19.278 --> 00:57:23.182
You are the only thing
in the world for me, Elena.

651
00:57:23.183 --> 00:57:28.177
So, tell me, what it is
that you think I did there?

652
00:57:29.557 --> 00:57:32.692
Your patients were war criminals.

653
00:57:32.693 --> 00:57:35.228
Monsters.

654
00:57:35.230 --> 00:57:38.400
Men who destroyed villages
and tortured people to death,

655
00:57:38.401 --> 00:57:40.855
all in the name of Spain, of Franco.

656
00:57:41.570 --> 00:57:46.905
And when they came to you,
you absolved them of all guilt.

657
00:57:46.907 --> 00:57:50.135
You purified them.

658
00:57:50.810 --> 00:57:54.113
Yeah, you came up
with that phrase, didn't you?

659
00:57:54.114 --> 00:57:56.447
You must have.

660
00:57:56.448 --> 00:58:02.889
"The Morales Institute
for Psychological Purification".

661
00:58:02.891 --> 00:58:05.590
Thousands of innocent people
murdered,

662
00:58:05.592 --> 00:58:08.262
and whenever their killers
felt pangs of guilt,

663
00:58:08.264 --> 00:58:10.399
they went to you,
and you told them,

664
00:58:10.400 --> 00:58:13.131
"Don't worry about it,
you had no choice.

665
00:58:13.133 --> 00:58:14.932
Get on with your life".

666
00:58:14.933 --> 00:58:17.168
Those men,
when they were brought to me,

667
00:58:17.170 --> 00:58:21.142
those "monsters" as you call them,
they were lost.

668
00:58:21.143 --> 00:58:23.143
So as a last hope,

669
00:58:23.144 --> 00:58:26.179
they brought them to me,
and yes, I purified them.

670
00:58:26.180 --> 00:58:28.183
To you, it is simple.

671
00:58:28.184 --> 00:58:29.648
They were evil men,

672
00:58:29.650 --> 00:58:34.043
and because I treated them,
I am evil, too. Hmm?

673
00:58:35.789 --> 00:58:38.925
But you forget something.

674
00:58:38.926 --> 00:58:43.179
These were the men who had won.

675
00:58:45.902 --> 00:58:49.969
They held the fate of Spain
in their hands,

676
00:58:49.970 --> 00:58:52.291
and they had tasted power.

677
00:58:53.573 --> 00:58:57.966
And what is the greatest power
that a man can have?

678
00:58:59.347 --> 00:59:02.181
It is to kill.

679
00:59:02.183 --> 00:59:06.720
And if you have killed a thousand,
what are a thousand more?

680
00:59:06.723 --> 00:59:10.991
If you have tortured one person,
is it too hard to torture another?

681
00:59:10.992 --> 00:59:12.125
No.

682
00:59:12.126 --> 00:59:16.063
It's easy. It's the easiest thing
in the world.

683
00:59:16.064 --> 00:59:19.332
This is what our country
was faced with.

684
00:59:19.334 --> 00:59:22.369
So what was I to do?

685
00:59:22.370 --> 00:59:25.237
Was I to leave these killers
as they were

686
00:59:25.239 --> 00:59:26.974
so they could go on killing?

687
00:59:26.975 --> 00:59:29.910
No, I could not agree to that.

688
00:59:29.911 --> 00:59:34.380
So, I purified them.

689
00:59:34.382 --> 00:59:39.218
I brought them back to life,

690
00:59:39.220 --> 00:59:43.418
and to humanity.

691
00:59:44.160 --> 00:59:48.360
How many people did I save?

692
00:59:48.361 --> 00:59:50.420
I think I saved many.

693
00:59:51.367 --> 00:59:55.969
So there you have it.
Do with it what you will.

694
00:59:55.971 --> 01:00:00.438
But you are no longer a teenager.

695
01:00:00.440 --> 01:00:04.042
The world is a very complex place.

696
01:00:04.043 --> 01:00:06.934
There is very little of it
that is black and white.

697
01:00:42.286 --> 01:00:44.916
Ah, so you're awake then.

698
01:00:44.917 --> 01:00:46.670
How do you feel?

699
01:00:47.322 --> 01:00:49.255
I'm fine.

700
01:00:49.257 --> 01:00:50.958
Marvelous.

701
01:00:50.960 --> 01:00:52.227
What time is it?

702
01:00:52.228 --> 01:00:54.194
Time to get started.

703
01:00:54.195 --> 01:00:55.945
Beyond that, who can say?

704
01:00:56.630 --> 01:00:58.565
Elena yesterday telephoned me

705
01:00:58.567 --> 01:01:00.401
and told me
about all your difficulties

706
01:01:00.403 --> 01:01:01.769
and asked if I could help.

707
01:01:01.771 --> 01:01:04.571
I said, "Well".
I was rather hesitant at first,

708
01:01:04.573 --> 01:01:07.774
because I've been retired
from the field for many years, but...

709
01:01:07.775 --> 01:01:10.177
Sorry, but you mentioned Elena?

710
01:01:10.178 --> 01:01:14.716
I am Joaquin Morales,
Elena's grandfather.

711
01:01:14.718 --> 01:01:16.984
Carmen, her mother,

712
01:01:16.985 --> 01:01:20.652
is always telling me
about your adventures.

713
01:01:20.654 --> 01:01:22.554
And now we finally meet,

714
01:01:22.556 --> 01:01:29.196
and, uh, it's a pity
it's in these... circumstances.

715
01:01:29.198 --> 01:01:32.100
I imagine you've heard
a great deal about me.

716
01:01:32.102 --> 01:01:34.567
Well, she said she stopped
talking to you a number of years ago

717
01:01:34.568 --> 01:01:36.304
because she heard
you were a fascist.

718
01:01:36.306 --> 01:01:37.773
A fascist!

719
01:01:39.607 --> 01:01:42.444
She's a very inventive girl.

720
01:01:42.445 --> 01:01:47.848
Por Dios, all this talk is just
torturing me from reason I came.

721
01:01:47.850 --> 01:01:52.353
I would like to make
a brief patient history of you,

722
01:01:52.355 --> 01:01:55.210
if that's all right with you.

723
01:01:56.560 --> 01:01:58.459
Sure, I've no choice.

724
01:01:58.461 --> 01:02:00.960
- Age?
- 34.

725
01:02:00.961 --> 01:02:03.262
Parents still alive?

726
01:02:04.301 --> 01:02:07.903
Father passed away
and mother lives in London.

727
01:02:07.905 --> 01:02:10.072
- Brothers and sisters?
- Nope.

728
01:02:10.073 --> 01:02:11.265
Hmm.

729
01:02:11.907 --> 01:02:15.075
Well, then, let's get started.

730
01:02:15.076 --> 01:02:17.411
- That's it?
- I beg your pardon?

731
01:02:17.412 --> 01:02:19.681
I thought psychiatrists
were supposed to ask you

732
01:02:19.683 --> 01:02:22.216
all sorts of questions
about your childhood experiences

733
01:02:22.217 --> 01:02:24.252
and relationships and all that stuff.

734
01:02:24.253 --> 01:02:26.980
Unnecessary. Exotic.

735
01:02:27.756 --> 01:02:30.192
You're not actually a psychiatrist,
are you?

736
01:02:30.193 --> 01:02:33.728
Well, I like to think of myself
as a...

737
01:02:33.730 --> 01:02:36.563
a scholar of the human spirit.

738
01:02:36.565 --> 01:02:39.167
Ah, that's lovely.

739
01:02:39.169 --> 01:02:41.204
Tell me a war story.

740
01:02:41.205 --> 01:02:43.704
- What?
- A war story.

741
01:02:43.705 --> 01:02:45.005
You must have many,

742
01:02:45.007 --> 01:02:47.107
but the first one
that comes to your mind.

743
01:02:47.108 --> 01:02:51.101
It's a bit early
in the fucking day for that.

744
01:02:54.918 --> 01:02:57.203
You're serious.

745
01:03:47.403 --> 01:03:51.361
I don't even know
if his family got the body back.

746
01:03:51.774 --> 01:03:55.276
Why do you think
this incident affected you?

747
01:03:55.278 --> 01:03:57.780
I dunno.

748
01:03:57.781 --> 01:03:59.816
Maybe I felt responsible.

749
01:03:59.817 --> 01:04:05.219
Well, perhaps if you hadn't
been there, he wouldn't have run.

750
01:04:05.221 --> 01:04:10.076
The soldiers
would not have fired, hmm?

751
01:04:11.195 --> 01:04:12.993
I suppose so.

752
01:04:12.995 --> 01:04:14.897
Well, that makes perfect sense.

753
01:04:14.899 --> 01:04:19.857
You feel that you're responsible
because to a great degree you are.

754
01:04:20.903 --> 01:04:24.173
You think I'm too harsh?

755
01:04:24.174 --> 01:04:26.442
How many people
have you told this story to, Mark?

756
01:04:26.444 --> 01:04:29.136
Four? Three? Two? Twenty?

757
01:04:29.880 --> 01:04:32.983
And what do they say?
"Oh, you mustn't blame yourself".

758
01:04:32.985 --> 01:04:35.617
"Oh, no, it was not your fault".

759
01:04:35.618 --> 01:04:39.210
"Oh, no, there was nothing
you could do". Am I correct?

760
01:04:39.823 --> 01:04:42.392
You have looked to others
for forgiveness,

761
01:04:42.393 --> 01:04:44.058
but, as you have discovered,

762
01:04:44.060 --> 01:04:46.496
this is something
they cannot give you.

763
01:04:46.497 --> 01:04:49.632
We cannot let go of the pain.
We have to carry it with us forever.

764
01:04:49.634 --> 01:04:53.738
That is what it means to live.

765
01:04:53.739 --> 01:04:58.541
Now, I can help you
to live with this pain.

766
01:04:58.543 --> 01:04:59.793
Look at me.

767
01:05:01.645 --> 01:05:04.748
I am 86 years old.

768
01:05:04.749 --> 01:05:07.783
I lost my entire family.

769
01:05:07.784 --> 01:05:10.288
I lost my parents,

770
01:05:10.290 --> 01:05:15.493
I lost my brothers and sisters,
and I lost my wife.

771
01:05:15.494 --> 01:05:19.094
And yet, I am still here.

772
01:05:19.096 --> 01:05:21.097
I can still smile,

773
01:05:21.098 --> 01:05:25.556
and the world is still...
a wonderful place.

774
01:05:31.143 --> 01:05:34.842
Elena will probably be here
before long.

775
01:05:34.844 --> 01:05:38.335
So you tell her I'm in the hotel.

776
01:05:41.887 --> 01:05:46.139
Oh, by the way, you know
you sleep very peacefully.

777
01:05:46.525 --> 01:05:49.725
There's not a movement,
not a wrinkle in your face.

778
01:05:49.726 --> 01:05:51.114
Just like a baby.

779
01:05:51.561 --> 01:05:53.447
That's a good thing, isn't it?

780
01:05:53.864 --> 01:05:55.198
No.

781
01:05:55.199 --> 01:05:58.567
If you were thrashing about
and muttering to yourself,

782
01:05:58.568 --> 01:06:01.836
it would mean the problem
is close by.

783
01:06:01.838 --> 01:06:03.638
But peacefulness?

784
01:06:03.640 --> 01:06:06.370
In a grown man,
that is not a good sign.

785
01:06:29.936 --> 01:06:31.991
Thank you.

786
01:06:32.771 --> 01:06:36.489
- I'll see you at home.
- Sure.

787
01:06:51.521 --> 01:06:54.459
12... 13...

788
01:06:54.460 --> 01:06:56.892
14... 15...

789
01:06:56.893 --> 01:06:59.463
16... 17...

790
01:06:59.465 --> 01:07:02.265
Excellent.
You're almost back to normal.

791
01:07:02.267 --> 01:07:04.701
I'm not. I'm moving
with less grace than you.

792
01:07:06.073 --> 01:07:07.322
Splendid.

793
01:07:07.672 --> 01:07:11.539
A sense of humor is always
one of the last abilities to return.

794
01:07:11.541 --> 01:07:12.929
That was the office.

795
01:07:14.211 --> 01:07:16.680
A Red Cross team in Kurdistan
just found David's things

796
01:07:16.682 --> 01:07:20.184
in a hotel room in Rawa...

797
01:07:20.185 --> 01:07:21.985
Rawanduz.

798
01:07:21.987 --> 01:07:23.577
Yeah.

799
01:07:24.021 --> 01:07:26.222
It's a town in the north.

800
01:07:26.223 --> 01:07:28.393
- It was our base.
- In the north?

801
01:07:28.395 --> 01:07:30.279
But I thought you told me
he went south.

802
01:07:30.695 --> 01:07:32.863
I don't know. He was...

803
01:07:32.864 --> 01:07:36.765
He was heading south, and I was
supposed to go back to Rawanduz,

804
01:07:36.766 --> 01:07:40.469
but I just headed straight for Turkey
when I had a chance, you know?

805
01:07:40.471 --> 01:07:43.667
Everything's still there.
His clothes, everything.

806
01:07:45.210 --> 01:07:47.745
Okay. What do we do?

807
01:07:47.747 --> 01:07:49.580
Is there anyone there
we can contact?

808
01:07:49.581 --> 01:07:50.647
I don't know.

809
01:07:50.648 --> 01:07:52.278
Think, Mark!
There must be someone!

810
01:07:52.280 --> 01:07:53.317
I don't know!

811
01:07:53.318 --> 01:07:56.480
This is not very helpful.
Not right now.

812
01:07:57.857 --> 01:08:00.857
What am I supposed
to tell Diane, huh?

813
01:08:00.858 --> 01:08:03.348
My God, what do I tell her?

814
01:08:04.863 --> 01:08:07.522
I have to get over there.
Call me if you think of anything.

815
01:08:14.674 --> 01:08:16.604
I'm afraid I don't...

816
01:08:16.606 --> 01:08:18.358
I don't understand.

817
01:08:20.546 --> 01:08:22.478
My best friend, David.

818
01:08:22.480 --> 01:08:26.578
We went to Kurdistan together,
but we split up.

819
01:08:29.221 --> 01:08:31.087
He should have been back
a week ago.

820
01:08:31.089 --> 01:08:33.440
And you're worried?

821
01:08:35.127 --> 01:08:37.761
A little bit, yeah.

822
01:08:37.763 --> 01:08:41.566
His wife is expecting a baby
any day.

823
01:08:41.568 --> 01:08:44.970
You know. But he's careful.

824
01:08:44.971 --> 01:08:48.664
It's not the first time one of us
has been late back.

825
01:08:50.242 --> 01:08:53.978
Hmm. And it's a war zone, of course.

826
01:08:53.980 --> 01:08:58.650
There are often many delays.

827
01:08:58.651 --> 01:09:01.812
But I don't think you should worry
about him. He'll be fine.

828
01:09:02.788 --> 01:09:06.883
And the most important thing is
to concentrate on your treatment.

829
01:09:07.727 --> 01:09:10.455
Which reminds me.

830
01:09:11.196 --> 01:09:16.866
I know very little
about the geography of Kurdistan.

831
01:09:16.868 --> 01:09:21.940
It was partitioned
by the English, wasn't it?

832
01:09:21.941 --> 01:09:25.311
Yeah. Any time you find a mess
in the world,

833
01:09:25.313 --> 01:09:27.198
it's usually thanks to them
or the French.

834
01:09:28.147 --> 01:09:33.641
Well, perhaps you could
draw a map for me?

835
01:09:36.288 --> 01:09:38.073
What, on the wall?

836
01:09:38.490 --> 01:09:40.024
Why not?

837
01:09:40.026 --> 01:09:43.262
You think it will clash
with the decor?

838
01:09:43.263 --> 01:09:46.650
Just a rough map.

839
01:09:49.135 --> 01:09:51.225
Jesus.

840
01:10:00.948 --> 01:10:03.047
And while you're doing it,

841
01:10:03.048 --> 01:10:06.278
perhaps another one
of your war stories.

842
01:11:06.680 --> 01:11:09.704
Sorry, I don't understand you.

843
01:12:51.653 --> 01:12:54.022
You know, the odds alone
would say that

844
01:12:54.023 --> 01:12:58.257
not even one of those skulls
belonged to a member of her family.

845
01:12:58.258 --> 01:13:00.524
So, in effect, I'd...

846
01:13:00.526 --> 01:13:04.129
...given her a bunch of strangers
to take home with her, you know?

847
01:13:04.130 --> 01:13:08.636
You are wrong about one thing.

848
01:13:08.638 --> 01:13:11.805
She knew well
that you were pretending.

849
01:13:11.806 --> 01:13:14.171
That is why she was grateful.

850
01:13:14.173 --> 01:13:17.477
That is why she thanked you.
For the charade.

851
01:13:17.478 --> 01:13:21.415
For the performance you put on
for her benefit.

852
01:13:21.416 --> 01:13:26.017
It's very complicated
being a survivor, you know?

853
01:13:26.019 --> 01:13:31.890
Sometimes you have to place
your faith in magic,

854
01:13:31.891 --> 01:13:36.962
or believe in things that you know
are not true if you want to continue.

855
01:13:36.963 --> 01:13:39.566
It's a kind of an instinct.

856
01:13:39.568 --> 01:13:43.537
If you wish to continue,
if you wish to survive,

857
01:13:43.538 --> 01:13:48.394
then you must bury the dead.

858
01:13:51.315 --> 01:13:56.451
Tell me, does the camera act
as a kind of filter for you,

859
01:13:56.452 --> 01:13:58.885
to distance you
from your surroundings?

860
01:13:58.887 --> 01:14:02.655
Sure. I suppose that's why a lot
of photographers have been killed

861
01:14:02.656 --> 01:14:04.625
throughout the years.

862
01:14:04.626 --> 01:14:06.326
Looking through the camera,
it's easy to forget

863
01:14:06.328 --> 01:14:08.093
that the things that are happening
in front of you

864
01:14:08.095 --> 01:14:09.561
are actually real, you know?

865
01:14:09.563 --> 01:14:12.933
Or sometimes it fails, huh?

866
01:14:12.934 --> 01:14:15.870
Sometimes the real world
takes over,

867
01:14:15.871 --> 01:14:20.156
and then, when there's no one
to help you, you're very lonely.

868
01:14:20.474 --> 01:14:22.909
Perhaps that is a reason
why you travel

869
01:14:22.910 --> 01:14:25.144
with all the other photographers, no?

870
01:14:25.146 --> 01:14:27.048
Suppose so.

871
01:14:27.049 --> 01:14:28.830
And to protect each other?

872
01:14:29.483 --> 01:14:31.303
Yeah, that, too.

873
01:14:31.487 --> 01:14:33.511
One more observation.

874
01:14:38.461 --> 01:14:42.062
I wouldn't call it an obsession,

875
01:14:42.064 --> 01:14:45.816
but why are you so concerned
about what happens to the dead?

876
01:14:46.634 --> 01:14:50.502
I noticed this when you were
talking about the boy from Beirut.

877
01:14:50.504 --> 01:14:52.573
At the end, you said,

878
01:14:52.574 --> 01:14:58.345
"I don't even know if his family
ever got his body back".

879
01:14:58.347 --> 01:15:04.444
What is so important to you
about the fate of the dead?

880
01:15:05.588 --> 01:15:08.121
Maybe superstition.

881
01:15:08.123 --> 01:15:10.025
Superstition?

882
01:15:10.027 --> 01:15:14.362
Yeah. You know, that the dead
should be returned to their families.

883
01:15:14.363 --> 01:15:16.429
That they shouldn't be left alone.

884
01:15:16.430 --> 01:15:19.431
More important to you
than those who survive?

885
01:15:19.432 --> 01:15:21.355
No.

886
01:15:22.204 --> 01:15:25.057
No, I don't know.

887
01:15:26.008 --> 01:15:29.010
Actually, I don't know what
you're trying to get at, Joaquin.

888
01:15:29.011 --> 01:15:30.676
I think you do.

889
01:15:30.678 --> 01:15:32.281
I said, I don't.

890
01:15:32.282 --> 01:15:34.313
What do you feel
when you see all these bodies

891
01:15:34.315 --> 01:15:36.318
lying around everywhere?

892
01:15:36.319 --> 01:15:38.151
Sorrow? Fear?

893
01:15:38.152 --> 01:15:41.955
Relief that you
are not amongst them,

894
01:15:41.957 --> 01:15:43.857
that you have survived?

895
01:15:43.859 --> 01:15:45.258
Fuck you.

896
01:15:45.260 --> 01:15:47.752
- Excuse me?
- I said, fuck you!

897
01:15:48.829 --> 01:15:50.965
I'm not one of your fucking
war criminals, all right?

898
01:15:50.967 --> 01:15:52.132
So just back off!

899
01:15:52.133 --> 01:15:53.535
You said you wanted to talk to me.

900
01:15:53.536 --> 01:15:55.594
I never fuckin' asked you
to come here!

901
01:15:56.239 --> 01:15:58.774
I was asleep, my hospital room,

902
01:15:58.776 --> 01:16:00.868
you walked in
and fucking woke me!

903
01:16:01.377 --> 01:16:03.345
Well, we can stop if you prefer.

904
01:16:03.346 --> 01:16:05.512
- Yeah, I need some sleep.
- Well, I'll take you home.

905
01:16:05.514 --> 01:16:07.504
No, you're grand.
Stay where you are.

906
01:17:05.741 --> 01:17:09.026
Ah, so you're awake.

907
01:17:09.212 --> 01:17:12.147
- What time is it?
- Up you get. Up.

908
01:17:12.148 --> 01:17:13.614
I need coffee first.

909
01:17:13.615 --> 01:17:18.313
Oh, I'll get the coffee.
You just start the exercises.

910
01:17:34.305 --> 01:17:36.704
Great, I'm cured.

911
01:17:36.705 --> 01:17:39.407
You should take a look
at the front page of the newspaper.

912
01:17:39.409 --> 01:17:42.640
I think it will be of interest to you.

913
01:17:45.782 --> 01:17:48.205
Jesus Christ.

914
01:17:48.653 --> 01:17:51.402
That fucking animal.

915
01:17:52.456 --> 01:17:56.156
A little strong, perhaps,
for your Celtic taste,

916
01:17:56.158 --> 01:18:00.253
but I think it will do you
a lot of good.

917
01:18:00.797 --> 01:18:03.565
The French call our way
of making coffee

918
01:18:03.567 --> 01:18:06.835
le jus des chaussettes,
which means...

919
01:18:06.836 --> 01:18:09.336
"Socks juice," I know.

920
01:18:09.338 --> 01:18:13.641
Jesus, you have a fucking answer
for everything, don't you?

921
01:18:13.642 --> 01:18:15.599
Thank you.

922
01:18:16.081 --> 01:18:20.277
The fellow that got this picture
probably stands to make a fortune.

923
01:18:20.617 --> 01:18:24.285
We would have been there a month,
he might have come in yesterday.

924
01:18:24.287 --> 01:18:26.014
But he was there
when this happened.

925
01:18:26.289 --> 01:18:29.039
- We had no idea we'd be this right.
- We?

926
01:18:29.859 --> 01:18:34.681
You mean you
and your friend, David?

927
01:18:35.733 --> 01:18:36.899
Yeah.

928
01:18:36.900 --> 01:18:38.025
Mm-hmm.

929
01:18:39.570 --> 01:18:44.708
I wonder if you would, perhaps,
do another little drawing for me.

930
01:18:44.709 --> 01:18:50.378
Another map of the area
where the accident took place.

931
01:18:50.380 --> 01:18:53.914
Not in minute detail, of course,

932
01:18:53.916 --> 01:18:59.113
but just the location
of the hill and the river, hmm?

933
01:18:59.857 --> 01:19:01.847
Come on, up! Up!

934
01:19:11.368 --> 01:19:13.495
All right.

935
01:19:18.709 --> 01:19:20.766
Let me see.

936
01:19:22.079 --> 01:19:25.503
Well...

937
01:19:25.650 --> 01:19:27.381
This is the hill.

938
01:19:27.383 --> 01:19:29.770
This is the river.

939
01:19:32.957 --> 01:19:36.058
This is where the explosion...

940
01:19:36.059 --> 01:19:38.027
...happened.

941
01:19:38.028 --> 01:19:39.361
And...

942
01:19:39.363 --> 01:19:42.897
somewhere here is
where I fell into the river,

943
01:19:42.899 --> 01:19:46.736
and somewhere down here
is where they found me.

944
01:19:46.738 --> 01:19:48.838
The Harir Cave
is somewhere over there.

945
01:19:48.839 --> 01:19:50.258
What of David?

946
01:19:52.243 --> 01:19:56.462
Where was he standing
when the explosion occurred?

947
01:20:03.289 --> 01:20:06.256
I've always known, Mark.

948
01:20:06.257 --> 01:20:09.458
I've always known since
the first time I heard his name

949
01:20:09.459 --> 01:20:11.188
and saw your face.

950
01:20:11.761 --> 01:20:15.821
And now you have to...
tell your story.

951
01:20:17.099 --> 01:20:19.400
It's not easy, I know.

952
01:20:19.401 --> 01:20:20.771
What I'm trying to say to you

953
01:20:20.773 --> 01:20:25.475
is that you are not as tough
as you think you are,

954
01:20:25.476 --> 01:20:29.571
and you cannot
carry this on forever.

955
01:20:30.949 --> 01:20:37.148
You must come back
to the people who love you.

956
01:20:40.359 --> 01:20:44.144
- David!
- Mister Walsh, are you coming?!

957
01:20:54.473 --> 01:20:57.574
Hey, we're not going
to make it this trip.

958
01:20:57.575 --> 01:21:01.327
All right. Listen, good luck.
Godspeed to ya.

959
01:21:13.892 --> 01:21:15.625
How much further do you think?

960
01:21:15.627 --> 01:21:19.162
Fucked if I know.
This is your bright idea, man.

961
01:21:19.164 --> 01:21:20.862
Tell me you're still
not angry at me?

962
01:21:20.863 --> 01:21:24.133
Nah. Nah, you're right.
It's time to get home.

963
01:21:24.135 --> 01:21:27.056
Jesus, it's beautiful, man.

964
01:21:50.092 --> 01:21:51.945
You know what?

965
01:22:20.527 --> 01:22:21.651
David!

966
01:22:21.726 --> 01:22:23.728
David!

967
01:22:23.729 --> 01:22:25.127
David!

968
01:22:25.128 --> 01:22:26.396
Don't shoot!

969
01:22:26.398 --> 01:22:27.919
David!

970
01:22:28.198 --> 01:22:31.802
Hey! Hey! Hey!

971
01:22:31.804 --> 01:22:32.860
Hey!

972
01:22:33.537 --> 01:22:35.538
Hey!

973
01:22:35.539 --> 01:22:36.962
David!

974
01:22:48.621 --> 01:22:52.817
No! I'm gonna fucking die!

975
01:23:13.613 --> 01:23:14.713
Mark!

976
01:23:14.714 --> 01:23:17.981
Mark! I'm gonna die!

977
01:23:17.983 --> 01:23:20.871
I'm gonna die!

978
01:23:22.022 --> 01:23:25.523
Mark, I'm gonna die!
I'm gonna die!

979
01:23:25.524 --> 01:23:27.376
Take me home!

980
01:24:09.336 --> 01:24:11.636
Mark, the chair.
Get us the chair.

981
01:24:11.638 --> 01:24:13.805
Take me to the hospital!

982
01:24:13.806 --> 01:24:15.056
Don't worry.

983
01:24:19.412 --> 01:24:20.401
Be calm.

984
01:24:27.920 --> 01:24:29.738
Vamos.

985
01:25:04.889 --> 01:25:07.013
Look at her.

986
01:25:14.566 --> 01:25:17.021
Would you like to hold her?

987
01:25:35.087 --> 01:25:37.373
I'm so sorry.

988
01:25:37.858 --> 01:25:39.606
Diane, I loved him so much.

989
01:25:45.066 --> 01:25:46.816
I know. He loved you, too.

990
01:25:49.336 --> 01:25:51.961
I wanted to tell you.

991
01:25:53.875 --> 01:25:56.624
I just didn't know how.

992
01:25:59.144 --> 01:26:01.135
Where did you bury him?

993
01:26:44.658 --> 01:26:48.829
Hey, where is Mark?

994
01:26:48.830 --> 01:26:52.082
I don't know.
He left a few minutes ago.

995
01:26:58.904 --> 01:27:00.427
Elena.

996
01:27:02.544 --> 01:27:04.998
Be nice to him.

997
01:27:06.214 --> 01:27:11.001
We don't know what it's like to go
to those places and come back.

998
01:27:12.053 --> 01:27:19.115
And anyway, be nice to him,
because he is back.

999
01:28:07.641 --> 01:28:10.610
I wanted to take him home.

1000
01:28:10.611 --> 01:28:12.963
I really did.

1001
01:28:13.846 --> 01:28:16.975
I kept saying...

1002
01:28:20.689 --> 01:28:26.683
"Don't worry, I'm taking you with me.
I'm taking you home".

1003
01:28:34.469 --> 01:28:36.664
I'm taking you home.

1004
01:28:37.739 --> 01:28:39.830
I'm taking you home.

1005
01:28:47.080 --> 01:28:49.537
I'd been walking for hours.

1006
01:28:50.517 --> 01:28:53.577
I came to a river.
There was no crossing.

1007
01:28:54.989 --> 01:29:00.688
I had no choice
but to jump in and just hope.

1008
01:29:34.929 --> 01:29:36.696
He was too heavy.

1009
01:29:36.697 --> 01:29:38.184
I was choking.

1010
01:29:39.533 --> 01:29:42.268
I couldn't breathe.

1011
01:29:42.270 --> 01:29:44.537
I couldn't breathe.

1012
01:29:44.538 --> 01:29:46.825
And so...

1013
01:29:48.010 --> 01:29:50.796
I let him go.

1014
01:30:23.580 --> 01:30:26.102
I'm sorry.

1015
01:30:30.945 --> 01:30:33.211
I love you.

1016
01:30:33.212 --> 01:30:35.463
I love you so much.
